<%# ** THE RULE FORM IS ITS OWN PAGE, ON ONE CATEGORY, IN THREE STEPS (two-shapes spec §5). **

    Henry, 2026-09-05: "Expanded should just show all rules and suggestions. The new rule thing
    should be a form we go to." So this is a page rather than a panel, it is reached at
    `/budgets/new?category_id=` from the category it is about, and it asks three questions in the
    order a person answers them:

      1. What is this rule for, and how much?   — "Set aside $___ for [the whole category ▾]"
      2. When is it needed?                     — Every period · By a date (+ repeats every N months)
      3. What kind of rule is this?             — Bill · Usage · Choice

    ** THE OWNER PICKER IS DELETED, AND THAT IS THE STRUCTURAL CHANGE (§5). ** Every door into this
    form names the category — the panel's "+ New rule for Groceries", a suggestion's "Write it →" —
    so the page is TITLED by it, its item select is that category's items, its chips are that
    category's suggestions, and `BudgetsController::NEW_NEEDS_A_CATEGORY` turns a bare
    `/budgets/new` back to the Budget page rather than offering a select that could send the rule
    somewhere the button did not promise. On the create path the owner rides in ONE hidden field;
    on an edit it is not on the wire at all (fix round 1 — M1).

    ** THE SECOND COLUMN IS THE PREVIEW, AND IT IS THE SERVER'S (§5). ** It says the rule back in
    words and in arithmetic, off ONE `ClaimCalculator` on the unsaved rule, refreshed into a Turbo
    Frame as the blanks change — see `_preview.html.erb`. Two columns at ≥1024px, one below.

    THE FORM SUBMITS WORDS, NOT COLUMNS. `schedule` is the radio pair and `repeats` the checkbox;
    `RuleForm` turns them into `basis`, `interval_months` and `anchor_date`, and turns them back for
    this form to render. Nothing here knows that a repeating dated rule is
    `monthly + interval N + anchor`.

    THE REVEALS ARE AN ENHANCEMENT AND NOT A GATE. Each conditional block renders with `hidden` set
    to the state the CURRENT choice implies, and `rule_form_controller.js` toggles it as the radios
    move; the server answers whatever the form submits either way. The controller CLEARS a field as
    it hides it (and puts the value back when it returns), which is why `RuleForm` can refuse a due
    date on a per-period rule rather than laundering it away.

    ** THE `<noscript>` RULE AND THE "Preview" BUTTON ARE BOTH DELETED (§12) ** — see the note above
    the grid. A browser with no JavaScript gets a form that SAVES and refusals under the control that
    caused them; what it does not get is a preview. %>
<% rule_form = @rule_form %>
<% budget = rule_form.budget %>
<% category = @category %>
<% chips = @chips || [] %>

<%# THE BREADCRUMB IS THE TRAIL THE USER CAME DOWN (§5): the Budget page, the category whose panel
    holds the button, and this rule. On an edit the last crumb is the rule's own name, because that
    is what the user clicked "Edit" on. %>
<% last_crumb = rule_form.persisted? ? budget_rule_name(budget) : "New rule" %>
<% title = rule_form.persisted? ? "#{budget_rule_name(budget)} — edit rule" : "New rule" %>
<% title = "New rule for #{category.name}" if category.present? && !rule_form.persisted? %>
<%= page_header(
      title: title,
      subtitle: "Change any blank. Nothing is saved until you press the button.",
      breadcrumbs: breadcrumb_trail(["Budget", budget_page_path], *[category&.name].compact, last_crumb)
    ) %>

<%# ** THE `<noscript>` REVEAL IS DELETED WITH THE PREVIEW BUTTON (§12). ** It forced every
    `[data-rule-reveal]` block visible so that a browser with no JavaScript got the whole form and a
    Preview button that worked by navigating. §12 rules that there is NO preview without JavaScript,
    which takes the button with it — and with the button gone the rule was propping up half a
    promise: a no-JS user still SAVES through the same server, and a shape the reveals hide is
    refused with the message under the control that chose it, which then re-renders revealed. What
    is lost is one round trip on that path; what is gained is that the form has exactly one story
    about which controls are on screen. %>
<div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-3 lg:gap-8 lg:items-start"
     data-controller="app--budget--rule-form"
     data-action="input->app--budget--rule-form#changed change->app--budget--rule-form#changed">
  <%# ** THE CONTROLLER IS ON THE GRID, NOT ON THE FORM, BECAUSE THE CHIPS ARE ABOVE IT AND THE
      PREVIEW IS BESIDE IT. ** A Stimulus action binds only inside its controller's own element, so
      a "Use this" button in a chip that sat OUTSIDE the form would never reach `#useChip` —
      measured: every blank stayed empty and the chip never said it was being used. It moved one
      level out with §12: the preview card is a SIBLING of this column, and the controller is what
      reveals it (`connect()`) and highlights it on every refresh, so the frame has to be inside the
      controller's element too. The form is a TARGET, and every read and every submit goes through
      that target rather than through `this.element`; the listeners are here because `input` and
      `change` bubble out of the form. %>
  <div class="lg:col-span-2 space-y-6">
    <%# ** THE CHIPS, ABOVE STEP 1 (§5). ** What the entries already suggest for this category, each
        one able to fill every blank below it. On an edit only the ones about THIS rule are here
        (`BudgetsController#chips_for`) — a chip about another rule would be a control that silently
        re-points the one the user opened. %>
    <% if chips.any? %>
      <section class="space-y-2" data-chips>
        <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">Your entries suggest</h2>
        <% chips.each do |suggestion| %>
          <%= render "chip", suggestion: suggestion %>
        <% end %>
      </section>
    <% end %>

    <%= simple_form_for rule_form,
        as: :budget,
        url: rule_form.persisted? ? budget_path(budget) : budgets_path,
        method: rule_form.persisted? ? :patch : :post,
        html: {
          class: "space-y-6",
          data: { "app--budget--rule-form-target": "form" }
        } do |f| %>
      <%# ERRORS WITH NOWHERE ELSE TO LAND. simple_form prints an attribute's error under that
          attribute's input, so an error on a field this form does not RENDER produces a 422 whose
          only visible text is "please review the problems below". `:base` is lifted here for that
          reason — an owner-less rule is a fact about the whole record and has no control at all,
          and neither does the category on either path now. %>
      <%= f.error_notification %>
      <% lifted = rule_form.errors[:base] + rule_form.errors[:category_id] %>
      <%= f.error_notification message: lifted.to_sentence if lifted.present? %>

      <%# ── 1. WHAT IS THIS RULE FOR, AND HOW MUCH? ─────────────────────────────────────────────
          §5's sentence, with the two blanks inside it: "Set aside $___ for [the whole category ▾]".
          The labels are there for a screen reader and the sentence is there for everyone else — the
          words around the controls ARE the question, so a second visible label above each would say
          it twice.

          ** THE SELECT IS THIS CATEGORY'S ITEMS AND NOTHING ELSE (§5), IN ONE STATEMENT. ** It used
          to be every item the user owns, stamped with its category and filtered in the browser as
          the owner picker moved; with one category in force there is nothing to filter between.
          "The whole category" is the default and the blank, and it MEANS something the user has to
          be told — the lane no other rule pays for — which is the hint underneath.

          `item_id` IS OWNERSHIP-SCOPED ON THE WAY IN (`BudgetsController#scoped_owners`) on all
          three doors (GET, POST and the preview): a stranger's id would write a rule against THEIR
          spending, so it 404s rather than rendering their item's name here. %>
      <section class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded border border-gray-100 p-4 space-y-3 sm:p-5" data-step="1">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">1. What is this rule for, and how much?</h2>

        <%# ** THE SENTENCE BREAKS BETWEEN ITS TWO CLAUSES AT 375, NOT INSIDE ONE (mobile pass,
            2026-09-06). ** `flex-wrap` over five children broke wherever the width ran out, which
            measured as `Set aside $[___] for` on line one and the select alone on line two — the
            preposition orphaned from the thing it points at. Binding `for` to the select in one
            wrapping span makes the only available break the one between the clauses, and the
            select then takes the line it is on: full width at 375, its own content width above it,
            where the whole sentence fits on one line as before. %>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
          <span>Set aside</span>
          <%= f.label :amount, "Amount", class: "sr-only" %>
          <span class="flex items-center gap-1">
            <span class="text-gray-500">$</span>
            <%= f.number_field :amount,
                class: "form-input w-32 tabular-nums",
                placeholder: "0.00",
                step: 0.01,
                min: 0,
                data: { "app--budget--rule-form-target": "amount" } %>
          </span>
          <span class="flex w-full items-center gap-2 sm:w-auto">
            <span>for</span>
            <%= f.label :item_id, "Pays", class: "sr-only" %>
            <%= f.select :item_id,
                options_from_collection_for_select(category ? category.items.order(:name) : [], :id, :name, rule_form.item_id),
                { include_blank: "the whole category" },
                class: "form-select w-full sm:w-auto",
                data: { "app--budget--rule-form-target": "item" } %>
          </span>
        </div>

        <p class="form-hint">
          <%= budget_amount_hint(budget) %>
          <% if category %>
            "The whole category" means anything in <%= category.name %> no other rule pays.
          <% end %>
        </p>

        <%# ** THE ROW WHOSE FIGURE IS IN A UNIT THE FORM IS ABOUT TO CHANGE (fix wave — MED-4;
            §5's ruling). ** A `monthly`-no-anchor rule reads back as "Every period" at its DIVIDED
            figure — the read-back preserves the money, not the number — so the box holds $120.00
            where the row says $260.00 a month, and this sentence is what accounts for the
            difference. On the drift-accept path the box holds neither figure but the one the
            entries measured, and the note says all three (see the helper).

            EXPLANATORY, NOT A WARNING, AND STYLED AS ONE: nothing here is wrong and there is nothing
            to defend against — `text-status-warning` was left over from the sentence this replaced
            ("change the amount if you mean that"), which asked the reader to guard themselves
            against their own form. %>
        <% if note = budget_monthly_conversion_note(rule_form, suggested: @suggested_amount) %>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600" data-monthly-conversion><%= note %></p>
        <% end %>

        <%# WHAT THE RULE SAYS TODAY, beside what a drift suggestion is proposing it should say.
            ** THIS IS THE ONE FIGURE ON THE PAGE THAT IS NOT IN THE FORM'S OWN UNIT. ** The box is
            per-period money on every path since the read-back divides (fix round 1's ruling), and
            `@current_amount` is the ROW's — a month's, on the one shape where those differ — so it
            is `RuleForm#amount_unit` that names it and not the record's phrase. Shown only when the
            two differ: on every other edit the field IS the current amount.

            ** AND NEVER BESIDE THE NOTE (fix wave — MED-4/T4(a)). ** The one shape where the row's
            unit differs from the box's is the one the note is about, and the note already names the
            row's monthly figure — so both rendered together on every converted edit, saying
            "$260.00 a month" twice in two sentences. The note is the fuller one; this line is what
            is left for every other row, where there is no conversion to explain. %>
        <% if @current_amount.present? && rule_form.amount.present? &&
              rule_form.amount.to_d != @current_amount.to_d && !rule_form.converted_from_monthly? %>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500" data-current-amount>
            Currently <%= number_to_currency(@current_amount) %>
            <%= rule_form.amount_unit || budget_rule_basis_phrase(budget) %>.
          </p>
        <% end %>

        <% if rule_form.errors[:amount].any? %>
          <p class="form-error">Amount <%= rule_form.errors[:amount].to_sentence %></p>
        <% end %>
        <% if rule_form.errors[:item_id].any? %>
          <p class="form-error"><%= rule_form.errors[:item_id].to_sentence %></p>
        <% end %>

        <%# THE OWNER, ON THE ONE PATH WHERE THERE IS NO ROW TO CARRY IT. An edit submits no category
            at all — `#update` does not permit the key — because the rule already has one. %>
        <%= f.hidden_field :category_id unless rule_form.persisted? %>
      </section>

      <%# ── 2. WHEN IS IT NEEDED? ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
          THE TWO SHAPES OF §2, AS TWO OPTION CARDS. "Every period" is a spending allowance that
          resets with the paycheck; "By a date" is money saved up toward a day — a bill or a goal,
          which are ONE shape — and it reveals the date and a "repeats every N months" checkbox.
          `RuleForm` maps the pair onto the three columns, and `Budget`'s own shape refusals land
          back here, under the control that chose them, rather than under a `basis` field this form
          does not have.

          ** "Every month" LEFT THE OPTIONS AND NOT THE MODEL (§5's ruling). ** `monthly` with no due
          date is a legal row and `SuggestionEngine` still writes one; it is not a shape people write
          by hand, so the form does not offer it and `RuleForm.from` reads such a row back as "Every
          period" with its amount shown per month. Saving it unchanged converts it — which is why
          both the note in step 1 and the preview's two-unit line exist.

          ** THE UNSPENT-MONEY STEP AND ITS TARGET FIELD ARE DELETED (§7). ** A fund IS a dated rule
          whose amount is its target, so "builds up toward $5,000" is written as "$5,000" in step 1
          and "by Jun 1, 2027" here. %>
      <section class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded border border-gray-100 p-4 space-y-3 sm:p-5" data-step="2">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">2. When is it needed?</h2>

        <% schedules = [
             ["per_period", "Every period", "a spending allowance that resets with your paycheck"],
             ["by_date", "By a date", "money saved up toward a day — a bill or a goal"]
           ] %>
        <div class="space-y-2">
          <% schedules.each do |value, label, help| %>
            <label class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded border border-gray-200 p-3 cursor-pointer hover:border-gray-300">
              <%= f.radio_button :schedule, value, class: "form-radio mt-1" %>
              <span class="flex-1">
                <span class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900"><%= label %></span>
                <span class="block text-xs text-gray-500"><%= help %></span>
              </span>
            </label>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <% if rule_form.errors[:schedule].any? %>
          <p class="form-error">When it is needed <%= rule_form.errors[:schedule].to_sentence %></p>
        <% end %>

        <%# ** KEEPS WHAT IT DOESN'T SPEND — THE FUND, BACK AS ONE CHECKBOX (§12). ** Henry,
            2026-09-06: "there was no option to have it grow endlessly (no limit)". It is a detail OF
            "Every period" and not a third option: the money still arrives every period, and the only
            question is what the boundary does to what is left.

            ** IT IS ALWAYS ON SCREEN AND DISABLED UNDER "By a date", RATHER THAN REVEALED. ** A
            reveal would hide the one control that says what happens to unspent money on the shape
            where the answer is most surprising; disabled-and-unchecked SAYS the answer instead — a
            dated rule's build-up is already defined by its date. The Stimulus controller clears and
            disables it as the radios move, and `RuleForm#keeps?` refuses it server-side on that
            schedule whatever arrives, so the two halves agree with nothing to keep in step by hand. %>
        <div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-gray-100"
             data-app--budget--rule-form-target="keepsField">
          <%# ** THE HINT SAYS THE SHAPE WITHOUT THE RETIRED NOUN (fix round — LOW). ** §12's own
              draft read "like a fund you add to every period", and "fund" is the word §7 took off
              every screen (§10.6: it survives only as a bank-account verb). The sentence says the
              same thing in the app's own vocabulary — an allowance, what the boundary does to it,
              and that nothing caps it. %>
          <%= f.input :keeps,
              as: :boolean,
              label: "Keeps what it doesn't spend",
              hint: "an allowance that keeps building — whatever you don't spend stays claimed, " \
                    "with no limit",
              input_html: { disabled: rule_form.schedule == "by_date" } %>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 pl-3 border-l-2 border-gray-100">
          <%# THE DATE. It rode HIDDEN beside the basis until Henry's real-use testing of 2026-08-20:
              `SuggestionEngine#next_due_on` walks the LAST PAYMENT forward by the interval, so a
              bill paid late once proposed a rule whose every future due date was late with it — and
              the amount on the same form was editable while the date that decides when the money is
              needed was not. %>
          <div data-rule-reveal
               data-app--budget--rule-form-target="anchorField"
               <%= "hidden" unless rule_form.schedule == "by_date" %>>
            <%= f.input :anchor_date,
                as: :date,
                html5: true,
                label: "Due",
                hint: "The day the money has to be there. Change it if the last payment was early or late." %>
          </div>

          <%# REPEATS — a checkbox rather than a third option, because "every 6 months" is a detail OF
              "by a date" and not a different answer to when the money is needed. Its own reveal is
              the interval below; two nested reveals would each stash and clear the other's inputs on
              the way past, so every reveal on this form is a leaf. %>
          <div data-rule-reveal
               data-app--budget--rule-form-target="repeatsField"
               <%= "hidden" unless rule_form.schedule == "by_date" %>>
            <%= f.input :repeats,
                as: :boolean,
                label: "Repeats every N months",
                hint: "leave this off for something you pay once" %>
          </div>

          <%# THE INTERVAL, revealed by the checkbox alone. §2's row 4 is
              `monthly + interval + anchor`; a form offering the N without the date could submit
              row 2 with an N > 1, which `shape_must_be_valid` refuses. %>
          <div data-rule-reveal
               data-app--budget--rule-form-target="intervalField"
               <%= "hidden" unless rule_form.schedule == "by_date" && rule_form.repeats? %>>
            <%= f.input :interval_months,
                as: :integer,
                label: "Comes round every (months)",
                input_html: { min: 1, step: 1 } %>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <%# ── 3. WHAT KIND OF RULE IS THIS? ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
          BILL, USAGE OR CHOICE (§3, Henry's ruling of 2026-09-04) — "bills are must pays, usage is
          like power bill (can be lowered by adjusting life), choice is like a restaurant". It is a
          fact about the RULE and not about the category, because one category can carry a fixed bill
          on one of its items and a discretionary catch-all beside it, and it decides the GIVE-WAY
          ORDER when free money goes below zero: choice gives way first, bill last. The preview says
          which, in a sentence, because that consequence is the whole reason the question is asked.

          REQUIRED, WITH NO RADIO PRESELECTED ON A HAND-MADE RULE. The column defaults to `usage` so
          an untyped row claims neither "must be paid" nor "up to you", but a form that pre-answered
          the question would be deciding what this person is willing to sacrifice. A proposal DOES
          arrive typed, and its radio is checked. %>
      <section class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded border border-gray-100 p-4 space-y-3 sm:p-5" data-step="3">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">3. What kind of rule is this?</h2>

        <% type_help = {
             "bill" => "must be paid — gives way last",
             "usage" => "a need you can trim",
             "choice" => "up to you — gives way first"
           } %>
        <div class="space-y-2">
          <% Budget.rule_types.keys.each do |type| %>
            <label class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded border border-gray-200 p-3 cursor-pointer hover:border-gray-300">
              <%= f.radio_button :rule_type, type, class: "form-radio mt-1" %>
              <span class="flex-1">
                <span class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900"><%= type.capitalize %></span>
                <span class="block text-xs text-gray-500"><%= type_help[type] %></span>
              </span>
            </label>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <% if rule_form.errors[:rule_type].any? %>
          <p class="form-error">Type <%= rule_form.errors[:rule_type].to_sentence %></p>
        <% end %>
      </section>

      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:justify-end gap-3">
        <%# Back where the rule is listed, which is the Budget page — the same destination
            BudgetsController sends a save to. %>
        <%= link_to "Cancel", budget_page_path, class: "btn btn-secondary" %>

        <%= f.submit rule_form.persisted? ? "Update rule" : "Create rule", class: "btn btn-primary" %>
      </div>

      <%# ** THE PREVIEW'S SUBMITTER, HIDDEN — THERE IS NO "Preview" BUTTON ANY MORE (§12). ** It was
          a visible secondary button beside Cancel, and it was the no-JavaScript path: pressing it
          navigated, and the whole page came back with the card updated. §12 deletes it. What the
          button DID is still needed, because it is what makes the request carry exactly the fields a
          save would carry — `formaction`, `formmethod`, `formnovalidate` and `data-turbo-frame` all
          ride on the SUBMITTER, and `requestSubmit(submitter)` is what applies them — so the element
          stays and only its visibility goes.

          A HIDDEN BUTTON IS STILL A VALID SUBMITTER: `requestSubmit` refuses a DISABLED one and says
          nothing about `hidden`, which only takes it out of the layout, the tab order and the
          accessibility tree. Verified in a real browser on this form (see the task report), because
          "still a valid submitter" is exactly the kind of claim that is true of the spec and false of
          some engine.

          `formnovalidate`, because a preview of a HALF-FILLED form is the thing the card is for:
          browser validation would refuse to submit exactly the states whose missing blanks it is
          supposed to name. %>
      <%= button_tag "Preview",
          type: :submit,
          name: nil,
          hidden: true,
          tabindex: -1,
          "aria-hidden": true,
          formaction: rule_form.persisted? ? preview_budgets_path(id: budget.id) : preview_budgets_path,
          formmethod: :post,
          formnovalidate: true,
          data: { turbo_frame: "rule_preview", "app--budget--rule-form-target": "previewButton" } %>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# THE PREVIEW COLUMN — sticky at ≥1024px, under the form below it (§5). The card inside it
      renders `hidden` and Stimulus reveals it on connect (§12), so a browser with no JavaScript
      gets no preview rather than a stale one it has no way to refresh. %>
  <aside class="mt-6 lg:mt-0 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
    <%= render "preview", preview: @preview %>
  </aside>
</div>
